  <h1>India’s Cybersecurity Landscape — Rising Threats and Policy Gaps</h1>
  <div><em>The Hindu | Date: 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>As India becomes a global digital powerhouse, its cybersecurity vulnerabilities have grown exponentially. In 2024, The Hindu reported rising cases of ransomware, phishing, and state-sponsored cyberattacks targeting critical infrastructure. Despite new initiatives, India’s cybersecurity ecosystem faces policy gaps, manpower shortages, and coordination challenges.
________________________________________
Current Cybersecurity Scenario
•	Incidents: CERT-In reported >14 lakh cybersecurity incidents in 2023.
•	Sectors Targeted: Banking, energy grids, telecom, and healthcare.
•	Global Ranking: India ranked among the top 10 countries most targeted by cyberattacks (2024, Check Point report).
________________________________________
Causes of Rising Threats
1.	Digital Expansion
o	850+ million internet users; growth of fintech, UPI, and e-governance.
o	Expanding attack surface.
2.	Geopolitical Factors
o	State-sponsored attacks linked to border tensions with China and Pakistan.
3.	Weak Infrastructure
o	Many critical utilities lack updated firewalls and intrusion systems.
4.	Low Awareness
o	Citizens fall prey to phishing, OTP frauds, and identity theft.
________________________________________
Government Initiatives
•	CERT-In (Indian Computer Emergency Response Team): Nodal agency for monitoring and response.
•	National Cyber Security Policy (2013): Outdated, under revision for 2024.
•	Cyber Surakshit Bharat: Awareness program for officials.
•	Data Protection Law (2023): Provides baseline for securing personal data.
•	International Cooperation: Cyber dialogues with US, EU, Japan.
________________________________________
Challenges
•	Policy Lag: Absence of updated comprehensive national cybersecurity policy.
•	Manpower Shortage: India needs ~1 million cybersecurity professionals; current availability <20%.
•	Fragmented Responsibility: Overlap between ministries, regulators, and agencies.
•	Private Sector Vulnerability: MSMEs lack resources for robust protection.
________________________________________
Strategic Significance
•	National Security: Cyberattacks on power grids or defence systems can cripple nation.
•	Economic: Losses from cybercrime estimated at $6 billion annually.
•	Global Standing: Strong cybersecurity essential for Digital India credibility.
________________________________________
UPSC Relevance
•	GS-III (Internal Security): Cyber threats, national security.
•	GS-II (Governance): Digital laws, institutional frameworks.
•	Essay:
o	“In the digital era, cybersecurity is national security.”
o	“Technology without safeguards is a double-edged sword.”
•	PSIR Paper II: Cyber power as an element of state power in IR.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Update Policy: Finalize National Cybersecurity Strategy 2024.<br/>2.	Build Manpower: Cybersecurity courses in universities, skill missions.<br/>3.	Strengthen CERT-In: Upgrade to proactive AI-based threat detection.<br/>4.	Public–Private Collaboration: Encourage industry partnerships in threat intelligence.<br/>5.	Citizen Awareness: Mass campaigns on digital hygiene and fraud prevention.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>India’s cybersecurity challenge is no longer peripheral — it is central to national security, economy, and governance. While institutional mechanisms exist, stronger policy coherence, manpower investment, and citizen awareness are crucial to building resilience. For UPSC aspirants, cybersecurity represents how technology, security, and governance intersect in the 21st century.</p>
